# Lesson 2: The Screen Layout

## Understanding the Three Thirds

You've changed the border. Now it's time to draw on the screen itself. But before you can draw a single pixel, you need to understand one of the Spectrum's most infamous quirks: **screen memory is not linear**.

The Spectrum's display memory is divided into three separate 2048-byte sections called "thirds". This layout makes horizontal drawing simple but vertical drawing complex. Every game programmer who's ever worked on the Spectrum has had to master this.

This lesson is the foundation for everything visual you'll ever do. Master this, and vertical movement makes sense. Skip it, and you'll hit mysterious bugs forever.

---

## The One-Minute Tour

- **Screen memory is NOT linear** - it's divided into three separate 2048-byte sections called "thirds"
- **Third 0:** `$4000-$47FF` contains character rows 0-7 (top portion of screen)
- **Third 1:** `$4800-$4FFF` contains character rows 8-15 (middle portion)
- **Third 2:** `$5000-$57FF` contains character rows 16-23 (bottom portion)
- **Within each third:** Add 32 bytes to move down one character row
- **Across third boundaries:** You must restart at the new third's base address
- **This layout is why** vertical movement seems complex - you can't just add bytes continuously

---

## Example Programme 1: Seeing the Three Thirds

Let's make the thirds visible by drawing a horizontal line at the start of each one:

```asm
; The Screen Layout - Lesson 002, Example 1
; Demonstrate the three screen thirds by drawing at their start addresses

        DEVICE ZXSPECTRUM48

        ORG $8000

Start:
        ; Draw horizontal line at start of Third 0 ($4000)
        LD HL,$4000            ; Top of screen (Third 0 start)
        LD B,32                ; 32 bytes = full width
DrawThird0:
        LD (HL),$FF            ; Solid line
        INC HL
        DJNZ DrawThird0

        ; Draw horizontal line at start of Third 1 ($4800)
        LD HL,$4800            ; Middle-ish of screen (Third 1 start)
        LD B,32
DrawThird1:
        LD (HL),$FF
        INC HL
        DJNZ DrawThird1

        ; Draw horizontal line at start of Third 2 ($5000)
        LD HL,$5000            ; Lower part of screen (Third 2 start)
        LD B,32
DrawThird2:
        LD (HL),$FF
        INC HL
        DJNZ DrawThird2

        ; Infinite loop to keep display visible
Freeze:
        JR Freeze

End:
```

**To run:**
```
RANDOMIZE USR 32768
```

![Three horizontal lines showing the three thirds of Spectrum screen memory](/images/sinclair-zx-spectrum/phase-1/tier-1/lesson-002/example-1.png)

**What you're seeing:** Three horizontal black lines at different heights, visually proving the screen is divided into thirds. The space between these lines represents the jump from one third to the next. You can't just add 32 bytes to get from the end of Third 0 to the start of Third 1 - you need to explicitly load the new address.

---

## How Screen Memory Is Organized

Here's how the 6144 bytes of screen memory are organized:

```
Screen Memory Map (6144 bytes total, $4000-$57FF)
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ THIRD 0: $4000-$47FF (2048 bytes)               │
│ Character Rows 0-7 (Scanlines 0-63)             │
│                                                  │
│  $4000: Row 0, Scanline 0 (32 bytes)            │
│  $4020: Row 1, Scanline 0 (32 bytes)            │
│  $4040: Row 2, Scanline 0 (32 bytes)            │
│  ...                                             │
│  $40E0: Row 7, Scanline 0 (32 bytes)            │
│  $4100: Row 0, Scanline 1 (32 bytes)            │
│  ...                                             │
│  $47E0: Row 7, Scanline 7 (32 bytes)            │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ THIRD 1: $4800-$4FFF (2048 bytes)               │
│ Character Rows 8-15 (Scanlines 64-127)          │
│  (Same pattern as Third 0)                       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ THIRD 2: $5000-$57FF (2048 bytes)               │
│ Character Rows 16-23 (Scanlines 128-191)        │
│  (Same pattern as Third 0)                       │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

Key Facts:
• Each third contains 8 character rows
• Each character row = 8 scanlines tall (8 pixels)
• Each scanline = 32 bytes wide (256 pixels)
• Within a third: +32 moves to next character row
• Across thirds: Must restart at new base address
```

**Character row vs scanline:**
- A **character row** is 8 pixels tall (one character height)
- A **scanline** is 1 pixel tall (one horizontal line)
- The Spectrum has 24 character rows (192 scanlines total)

---

## Example Programme 2: Drawing a Vertical Line

Now let's draw a vertical line that goes through all three thirds correctly. This requires nested loops - one for character rows, one for scanlines within each row:

```asm
; The Screen Layout - Lesson 002, Example 2
; Draw a vertical line correctly through all three thirds

        DEVICE ZXSPECTRUM48

        ORG $8000

Start:
        ; Draw vertical line at column 16 through ALL three thirds
        ; Need to draw all 8 scanlines in each of 8 character rows in each third

        ; Third 0: Character rows 0-7
        LD HL,$4010            ; Start: Third 0, char row 0, scanline 0, col 16
        LD B,8                 ; 8 character rows in this third
DrawThird0Row:
        PUSH BC                ; Save character row counter
        PUSH HL                ; Save row start address
        LD B,8                 ; 8 scanlines per character row
DrawThird0Scanline:
        LD (HL),$80            ; Draw single pixel (bit 7)
        INC H                  ; Next scanline (+256 bytes)
        DJNZ DrawThird0Scanline
        POP HL                 ; Restore row start address
        LD DE,32
        ADD HL,DE              ; Move to next character row
        POP BC                 ; Restore character row counter
        DJNZ DrawThird0Row

        ; Third 1: Character rows 0-7 (screen rows 8-15)
        LD HL,$4810            ; Start: Third 1, char row 0, scanline 0, col 16
        LD B,8
DrawThird1Row:
        PUSH BC
        PUSH HL
        LD B,8
DrawThird1Scanline:
        LD (HL),$80
        INC H
        DJNZ DrawThird1Scanline
        POP HL
        LD DE,32
        ADD HL,DE
        POP BC
        DJNZ DrawThird1Row

        ; Third 2: Character rows 0-7 (screen rows 16-23)
        LD HL,$5010            ; Start: Third 2, char row 0, scanline 0, col 16
        LD B,8
DrawThird2Row:
        PUSH BC
        PUSH HL
        LD B,8
DrawThird2Scanline:
        LD (HL),$80
        INC H
        DJNZ DrawThird2Scanline
        POP HL
        LD DE,32
        ADD HL,DE
        POP BC
        DJNZ DrawThird2Row

        ; Infinite loop to keep display visible
Freeze:
        JR Freeze

End:
```

**To run:**
```
RANDOMIZE USR 32768
```

![Vertical line drawn correctly through all three thirds](/images/sinclair-zx-spectrum/phase-1/tier-1/lesson-002/example-2.png)

**What you're seeing:** A single continuous vertical line down the center of the screen. No gaps, no breaks. This is the correct way to handle vertical drawing on the Spectrum.

**The critical technique:**
- Outer loop: 8 character rows per third (move by +32)
- Inner loop: 8 scanlines per character row (move by +256, which is INC H)
- We restart at the base of each third (`$4010`, `$4810`, `$5010`)

---

## Address Calculation for Any Position

To calculate the address for any screen position:

```
Address = ThirdBase + (CharRow * 32) + Column

Where:
  ThirdBase = $4000 (rows 0-7)
            = $4800 (rows 8-15)
            = $5000 (rows 16-23)

  CharRow = 0-7 (which character row within the third)
  Column  = 0-31 (horizontal position)
```

**Examples:**

- **Top-left corner (row 0, col 0):** `$4000 + (0*32) + 0 = $4000`
- **Middle of screen (row 12, col 16):** `$4800 + (4*32) + 16 = $4890`
- **Bottom-right corner (row 23, col 31):** `$5000 + (7*32) + 31 = $57DF`

This formula gives you scanline 0 of a character row. To access other scanlines within that row, add `scanline * 256`.

---

## Movement Rules Within Screen Memory

### Horizontal Movement (Easy)

Moving within the same scanline:
- **Right:** `INC HL` (+1 byte)
- **Left:** `DEC HL` (-1 byte)

### Vertical Movement (Requires Care)

Moving down one character row **within the same third:**
```asm
LD DE,32
ADD HL,DE       ; +32 bytes
```

Moving down one scanline **within the same character row:**
```asm
INC H           ; +256 bytes
```

Moving down one character row **across third boundary:**
```asm
; At end of Third 0, row 7
LD HL,$47E0
; Can't just ADD 32!
; Must restart at Third 1 base:
LD HL,$4800     ; Start of Third 1
```

---

## Why This Layout Exists

**Historical reason:** Reduces hardware complexity.

In 1982, the Spectrum's video circuitry needed to be as simple as possible. The three-thirds layout aligns with how character attributes (colours) are stored separately - one attribute byte controls an 8×8 pixel block. This structure made the video hardware simpler and cheaper.

**Practical effect:** Horizontal drawing is simple (just increment), but vertical drawing requires understanding which third you're in and handling boundaries carefully.

---

## Game Integration: Why This Matters

Every game that moves objects vertically must handle screen thirds:

- **Moving the snake head down:** Can't just add bytes continuously - must detect third boundaries
- **Drawing vertical walls:** Must draw in three separate loops (one per third)
- **Scrolling graphics:** Must handle wrap-around at third boundaries
- **Collision detection:** Must account for non-linear addresses when checking positions

**The pattern you'll use constantly:**
```asm
; Draw/move in Third 0 (rows 0-7)
; Draw/move in Third 1 (rows 8-15)
; Draw/move in Third 2 (rows 16-23)
```

---

## Common Mistakes to Avoid

**Mistake 1: Assuming linear addresses**
```asm
LD HL,$4000
LD BC,32
ADD HL,BC        ; This moves down ONE character row
                 ; NOT one scanline!
```

**Mistake 2: Forgetting third boundaries**
```asm
; At row 7 (end of third 0)
LD HL,$47E0
LD BC,32
ADD HL,BC        ; Now at $4800 (Third 1 start)
                 ; This is row 8, NOT row 8 of third 0!
```

**Mistake 3: Wrong vertical movement calculation**
```asm
LD HL,$4000
LD BC,256
ADD HL,BC        ; This jumps to $4100
                 ; Which is scanline 1 of all rows in Third 0
                 ; NOT the next visible row!
```

---

